packages/main/src/core/process-fact-registry.ts:1465:    rationale: 'Typed from/to phase transitions bind attempt, review round, materialHash, and storyFilesHash so metadata-only rollback counts are mechanically derived. The gated PLAN->INIT reopen records three further durable audit semantics on the same append-only, appended-only-on-success event: transitionType: "reopen" marks the backward reopen so it is distinguishable from a forward transition, reason carries the operator reason already validated at the REOPEN remap (non-blank, at least REOPEN_REASON_MIN characters) so a validated reason is never dropped, and reopenAuditId carries the recovery idempotency key. All three are strictly additive and are set ONLY on a REOPEN; every ordinary transition must omit them and keep its exact prior event shape. RECOVERY SEMANTICS (B-70a6 F-004): the phase write and this event append are two separate fs operations, so a crash between them once left a committed reopen with NO audit record — unrecoverable, because REOPEN only fires FROM PLAN and the story had already left the only phase that could retry it. The reopen therefore writes a pendingReopenAudit recovery record (auditId, verbatim reason, fromPhase, at, materialHash, storyFilesHash, reviewRound) onto the story index in the SAME atomic writeStory rename that persists phase=INIT, so the reason is durable the instant the reopen commits; reconcilePendingReopenAudit then emits any missing event at the next ORCHESTRATED transition and clears the record, narrowed further by the OUT-OF-BAND PHASE MUTATIONS and CONCURRENCY RESIDUAL paragraphs below and never on an arbitrary read-only command. Recovery is idempotent by exact reopenAuditId identity, never by reason text: a missing event is appended exactly once, an already-audited record is cleared without appending, and the clear happens only after the event is durable. The record can only exist because the phase write it rode committed, so an aborted or refused reopen leaves neither record nor event and nothing can later fabricate a transition that did not happen. The record and reopenAuditId are audit-only recovery bookkeeping, not gate evidence: no gate consumes them, and the convergence counters above stay derived from the typed from/to phases. OUT-OF-BAND PHASE MUTATIONS (B-70a6 F-005): runStoryTransitionInner is NOT the only writer of story.phase, so the F-004 reconciliation at its entry does not by itself make the invariant framework-wide. Two production sites mutate story.phase directly through store.writeStory: story_discard (handlers/worktree.ts) and the story_reconcile zombie repair (handlers/story-query.ts). Both write a TERMINAL phase (DISCARDED / DONE) that has no outgoing machine edge, and story_discard additionally force-deletes the worktree and branch, so an unaudited reopen there was lost PERMANENTLY rather than deferred to the next transition. Both sites must therefore call reconcileReopenAuditBeforeOutOfBandPhaseChange BEFORE their destructive steps and before their phase write. That rescue reads the record from the DOOMED copy when necessary, because cli/runtime-root.ts redirects story_discard to the MAIN root while the reopen is cwd-gated into the worktree, so the handler store cannot see the record; it then appends the event to the SURVIVING store through the same reconcilePendingReopenAudit, keeping ONE append site and the same exact-auditId idempotency. The rescue never blocks the operation: discarding or reconciling a story stays legitimate, and every failure is surfaced as a warning instead of thrown, including a doomed copy that exists but cannot be read (corrupt index.json, permission error); the single deliberate silence is a doomed copy that holds no story metadata at all, which is a no-op rather than a failure. Declared residuals: the record on the DOOMED copy is deliberately not cleared, because writing into a worktree that is about to be status-checked could turn it dirty and refuse a non-force discard, 